  "text_md": "TALISHE. Fabulous. This word occurs in Palsgrave's Acolastus, 4to. 1540. TALL. (1) Explained by Junius, \" obedient, ob-\n\nTALL (1) Explained by Junius, ohedient, obSee the Glossary to Urry's Chaucer, p. 81.\n\nAither broght unto the place\n\nYtvaine and Gawin, 3158. thar was,\n\nAnd after mete it The children pleide at the taloas. Beves of Hamtoun\n\n(2) Valiant bold ; fine This is a common word in old plays. great . very\n\nBeves of Hamtoun, P. 145\n\nThey leapIng overboord amidst the billowes, We pluck'd her up (unsunke) like stout tall fellows.\n\nTALWHE. Tallow. TAM. The\n\nTAMaRA.\n\nThe abbr. of pr. n. Thomasine.\n\nTAMARA. A compound of spices:\n\nTALL-BOYS. High cups or glasses. Grose savs, bottles or two-quart pots. TALLE. To mock. {A.-S.)\n\nTAME. (1) To broach or taste   liquor. To tamne; tap, dolium relinere; Coles .\n\nTALLE. To mock.\n\n(A-s) Urarmed were the paiens alle, Our folk hem gun to talle.\n\nNowe to weete our mouthes tyme wcre, This fagette will tame, yf thou reade\n\nUS.\n\nArthour and Merlin, P. 257\n\nChester Plays, 1. 124\n\n(2) To cut to divide. West.\n\nTALLEE. When they hale aft the sheate of maine fore-sailes, say, Tallee aft the sheate; Taylia, Reliq- Anihey\n\nTAME-GOOSE A foolish fellow . Isay cast away yea, utterly cast away upon a noddy, ninny-hammer, tame-goose; The Case\n\nTALLICHE. The same as Tally (6). TALLIT. A hayloft. West. \"\n\nTAMER A team of horses. Norf:\n\nWhen the prisoner came in he was watcherd, which shewed he had not been all night in the tallitr TALL-MEN.\n\nTAMINE. A sort of woollen cloth.\n\nTAMLIN. A miner's tooL Cornw.\n\nTAMMY. Glutinous, or sizy. Cumb.\n\nTAMPIN. pellet. long\n\nTALL-MEN. Dice s0  loaded as to come up with high numhers. A cant term.\n\nor tampins of linncn clowtes, or such like stuffe, sharpe pointed like .suger-loves : which iowipiw* are called of the physitians in Latine pessi, and being annointed with the ointment aforesaid, thrust them up into the horsses nostrils, and let them abide therein a pretty whilk ; then pul them out, and you shal see such abundance of matter come forth at his nose as is marvellous to behold.\n\ncake of   tallow tallow made up in the form of a cake_ Var. dial.\n\nTALLOW-CATCH. Same as Keech (2)\n\nTALLOW-CRAPS:. See (1) Craps\n\nTALLOW-HIUED. Pale as tallow. North. Burton uses the phrase tallow-faced .\n\nTALLOW-LAFE. MS. Dict.\n\nCongiarium, 1500.\n\nTopselfs Four-Footed Beasts, 1C07, p. 372. TAMPING -IRON. A tool\" used for beating down the earthy substance in the charge used for blasting. Comw. TAMPION. A piece of wood fitted to the\n\n\" Tall woode, pacta wodde to make byllettes of, taillee\" Palsgrave. The term is still used in Kent. TALLY. A terra playing when\n\nTAMPION. piece of fitted to the mouth of a gun. Tampyon for a gon, tampon,\" Palsgrave; subst. f. 69. large\n\nTALLY. (1) A term in playing   ball, when the number of aces on both sides is North. equal.\n\n2) To reckon. See Becon's   Works, p. 134.\n\nUnadvisedly gave fire to a pcece charged with a pellet   insteede of tampion, the which llghting on the palaice wall, ranne through one of   the prlvie lodglngs, and dld no further harmc.\n\nby the hundred, bne is thrown out after each hundred ; that is called the tally. The number of tallies of course shows the number of hundreds. They are given in to the purchaser. Hunter. A kind\n\nLambardes Perambulatlon, 1596, p. 433. TAN. (1) Taken.\n\nWhen pese was cryed and tan, day\n\n(4 ) A kind of small ship.\n\nKyng Ardus was a yuyfulle man.\n\n(5) A company or division of   voters   at an election . Somerset .\n\nMS: Cantab.\n\nBaptem the first is holden than,\n\n(6) Stoutly boldly .\n\nThat falleth at the fonte bc tan.\n\nMS. Sloan . 1785 , f.34.\n\nSeemly decently ; elegantly.\n\n# (2; To entice. (A.-S.) Thefende of\n\nTALME. To become dumb ?\n\nhelleagayn skylle Put in hir a harde wille Hur fadur luf to wynne ; And also temped was that man His owne doujter for to tan. To do a dedly synne. MS. Cantab. Ff.\n\nHur fadur nere-hande can talme,\n\nSoche sweme hjs harte can swalme\n\nLe Bone Florence of Rone, 769\n\n1 donke upon David. til mi tongc talmes\n\nIne rendrede nowt sithen men beren palmes\n\nIs it also mlkel sorwe. in song s0 1s in Balmes\n\nMS , Cantab. Ff. 48, f. 43.\n\nThen. Far. dial. To dun. To\n\n292. TALSHIDES. \" One pound of white lights, ten talshides, eight faggotts,\" Ord. and Reg. p. 162.\n\nTo dun. (5) To beat. Var. dial.\n\n(6) A twig, or smnall switch. Lanc.\n\nformerly for torturing. \"To pinch or tanacle with tongs, with pincers or tanacles,\" Florio, p. 552, ed. 1611. 54\n\nTALT. Pitched. There was\n\nThere was talt many pavyloun TAIVACE. kind of  buckler or shield, bent on each and in the middle. side, rising A mikel rownd talvace.\n\nOf riche sendel and siclaloun.\n\n",
